## Context

The AIDE pipeline defines a sequence of phases (spec, research,
synthesize, plan, build, QA/fix, align, refactor), each described by a
slash command that tells the orchestrating agent what to do. But the
commands have no agents to delegate to — they say "spin up a Planner
agent" without shipping one. Users must define their own agents or
rely on the orchestrator to inline the work, which defeats the
fresh-context-per-phase architecture that makes the pipeline reliable.
AIDE also ships read-only investigator agents that are not bound to a
phase but support the same pipeline (e.g., codebase exploration,
intent-tree navigation); these install alongside the pipeline agents
because they share the canonical-agent delivery shape.

Claude Code agents are `.md` files in `.claude/agents/` with YAML
frontmatter (name, description, tools, model, mcpServers, color, etc.)
and a Markdown body that becomes the agent's system prompt. Agents cannot
nest — they return results to the caller. Up to 10 can run simultaneously.
The orchestrator delegates by naming the agent in the prompt or via
`@agent-<name>` mention.

AIDE is opinionated about the knowledge layer: the pipeline depends on
a brain that persists research and retros, and certain agents declare
brain access in their frontmatter. Brain configuration is user-owned —
the host's `brain.aide` is the single editable source of truth, and the
package never enumerates or branches on what's wired there. Agents
reference the brain by the fixed `brain` MCP key regardless of how the
user configured their `brain.aide`. Host projects bring their own vault
and their own prose instructions; the agents teach the workflow
pattern, not our content.

## Strategy

**One agent per pipeline role, not per phase.** Each canonical pipeline
agent maps to a distinct role; the implementor serves both build and
fix phases — same role, same prompt, different inputs. The domain expert
and strategist are separate agents because their brain-access patterns
differ (write-heavy vs read-heavy) and their reasoning demands differ
(volume research vs deep synthesis). Read-only investigators (e.g., the
explorer) are not bound to a single phase — they support the pipeline
across phases without writing files.

**Agents declare MCP servers by name reference, not inline config.** An
agent that needs brain access declares `mcpServers: [brain]` — the
literal `brain` is the fixed MCP key every host project wires under in
`.mcp.json`, populated from their `brain.aide`. The fixed key is what
makes the agent files portable: the same agent file ships byte-faithful
to every host, and each host's own `brain.aide` decides what sits
behind the key. The agent never asks "which brain"; it asks the entry
keyed `brain`, and the user's configuration answers. There is no
package-side notion of "backend" the agent could name even if it wanted
to — configuration variance lives entirely in the user's `brain.aide`,
never in the agent file's frontmatter.

**Model assignments optimize for task shape.** Opus for roles that require
deep reasoning under ambiguity (spec-writer distilling intent, strategist
synthesizing research, architect making structural decisions, aligner
resolving drift, auditor evaluating refactor targets). Sonnet for roles
that require throughput and pattern-matching (domain-expert doing volume
web research, implementor executing plans, QA doing systematic checking,
read-only investigators doing trace-and-report work). The per-agent
assignment lives in each canonical agent file's frontmatter.

**The study-playbook skill ships as a generic template.** It teaches the
hub-navigation pattern (read index → identify sections → drill into child
notes → follow wikilinks) but points at the host's own `coding-playbook/`
vault path. The host builds their own playbook; the skill teaches them how
to consume it. The architect and auditor agents' prompts reference this
skill by name.

**Agent files flow through the initContent chokepoint.** Canonical agent
`.md` files live in the repo at `.claude/agents/`. The `initContent` module
registers them in DOC_PATHS. The `installAgents` helper reads them through
`readCanonicalDoc()` and writes them to the host's `.claude/agents/`.
Same byte-faithful copy pattern as methodology docs and commands.

**Phase commands are updated to name agents explicitly.** Each command's
delegation instruction changes from "spin up a Planner agent" to "delegate
to the `aide-spec-writer` agent" with an `@agent-aide-spec-writer` mention
pattern. The command owns the what; the agent owns the how.

## Good examples

A fresh install run — through either the `aide_init` MCP tool or
the npx CLI — reports each canonical agent file from the registry
alongside everything else:

> ✓ aide-spec-writer: Created
> ✓ aide-domain-expert: Created
> ✓ aide-strategist: Created
> ✓ aide-architect: Created
> ✓ aide-implementor: Created
> ✓ aide-qa: Created
> ✓ aide-aligner: Created
> ✓ aide-auditor: Created
> ✓ aide-explorer: Created
> ✓ study-playbook skill: Created

A re-run on the same project:

> - aide-spec-writer: Already exists
> - aide-domain-expert: Already exists
> ...

A host project that has customized `aide-architect.md` with project-specific
conventions re-runs the install action and sees the architect reported as
"Already exists" — their customizations survive.

The `/aide:plan` command says: "Delegate to the `aide-architect` agent.
Provide it the path to the `.aide` spec and let it produce `plan.aide`."
The agent's own prompt handles the rest — reading the spec, consulting the
playbook via study-playbook, scanning the codebase, writing the plan.

## Bad examples

An agent file that hardcodes a personal vault path, or names the brain
MCP entry under any key other than the literal `brain` (a
connector-flavored or project-flavored key derived from whatever the
user happens to have wired today) instead of the fixed `brain` key.
Both violate the fixed-`brain`-key contract: the agent file is
supposed to ship byte-faithful to every host and ask the entry keyed
`brain` for brain access, with the host's own `brain.aide` deciding
what answers. A connector-flavored name couples the agent file to
whatever launcher this user happens to have wired today and breaks
portability across users whose `brain.aide` files configure the brain
differently. The agent fails on first brain access for any host whose
`.mcp.json` doesn't happen to expose the same key.

An agent prompt that includes the full study-playbook navigation
instructions inline instead of referencing the skill. When the skill
template is updated, the agent's inline copy drifts.

A phase command that still says "spin up an Architect agent" without
naming `aide-architect`. The orchestrator doesn't know which agent file
to use and falls back to inlining the work in the main context.

An install that installs agents but doesn't install the study-playbook skill.
The architect agent references the skill in its prompt, the skill doesn't
exist, and the agent skips playbook consultation entirely.
